a-5","type":"web_search_call","status":"completed","action":{"type":"open_page","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borobudur"}},{"id":"ws_dbceb994-f6af-9594-9cbd-1a8dd0856f24_call-fcaafbd6-2879-499c-9800-90179d2e401a-6","type":"web_search_call","status":"completed","action":{"type":"open_page","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angkor_Wat"}},{"id":"ws_dbceb994-f6af-9594-9cbd-1a8dd0856f24_call-fcaafbd6-2879-499c-9800-90179d2e401a-7","type":"web_search_call","status":"completed","action":{"type":"open_page","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/592/"}},{"id":"ws_dbceb994-f6af-9594-9cbd-1a8dd0856f24_call-fcaafbd6-2879-499c-9800-90179d2e401a-8","type":"web_search_call","status":"completed","action":{"type":"search","query":"Guinness World Records Angkor Wat largest religious structure","sources":[]}},{"id":"tco_dbceb994-f6af-9594-9cbd-1a8dd0856f24_call-fcaafbd6-2879-499c-9800-90179d2e401a-6","summary":[],"type":"reasoning","status":"completed"},{"id":"tco_dbceb994-f6af-9594-9cbd-1a8dd0856f24_call-fcaafbd6-2879-499c-9800-90179d2e401a-5","summary":[],"type":"reasoning","status":"completed"},{"id":"tco_dbceb994-f6af-9594-9cbd-1a8dd0856f24_call-fcaafbd6-2879-499c-9800-90179d2e401a-7","summary":[],"type":"reasoning","status":"completed"},{"id":"tco_dbceb994-f6af-9594-9cbd-1a8dd0856f24_call-fcaafbd6-2879-499c-9800-90179d2e401a-8","summary":[],"type":"reasoning","status":"completed"},{"content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"**1. Borobudur as the largest Buddhist temple (single monument) and its base dimensions:**\n\nBorobudur is widely recognized as the largest Buddhist temple in the world by footprint and scale as a single monument/structure (a massive stupa-temple complex).[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borobudur)[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borobudur) This is a standard description in archaeological and tourism sources; it is not a formal Guinness record but a scholarly consensus based on its size, construction, and Buddhist function. **REPORTED/VERIFIED** (primary/scholarly sources including Wikipedia summaries of archaeological consensus and site descriptions).\n\nBase dimensions: Approximately **123 m × 123 m** (with the original foundation often cited as ~118 m on each side). It rises to about 35 m in height.[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borobudur)[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borobudur) **VERIFIED** (Wikipedia citing architectural measurements; consistent across multiple sources including Britannica references to its scale). The UNESCO listing describes it as a pyramidal base with five concentric square terraces.[[2]](https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/592/)\n\n**Borobudur UNESCO status:** It is part of the **Borobudur Temple Compounds** UNESCO World Heritage Site (inscribed 1991, criteria i, ii, vi). **VERIFIED** (official UNESCO page).[[2]](https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/592/)\n\n**2. Angkor Wat as the largest religious monument/structure and area distinctions:**\n\nAngkor Wat (the single temple complex) is recognized as the **largest religious monument/structure in the world** by land area/footprint, per Guinness World Records.[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angkor_Wat)[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angkor_Wat) This is frequently cited in Britannica and other sources. **VERIFIED** (Guinness recognition as reported in reliable secondary sources like Wikipedia; also Britannica).[[4]](https://www.britannica.com/topic/Angkor-Wat)\n\n**Angkor Wat temple enclosure (single temple):** Site area of **162.6 hectares** (1.626 km² or ~402 acres), enclosed by an outer wall and moat.[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angkor_Wat)[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angkor_Wat) This refers specifically to the temple proper within the ancient capital of Angkor.\n\n**Angkor Archaeological Park (whole complex):** Approximately **400 km²** (including forested areas and dozens of other temples/ruins from the Khmer Empire capitals, 9th–15th centuries).[[5]](https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/668/)[[5]](https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/668/)\n\n**Clear distinction:** Angkor Wat is the single iconic temple/monument (the one with the famous towers and galleries). The Angkor Archaeological Park is the much larger protected archaeological zone containing Angkor Wat plus many other sites (e.g., Angkor Thom, Bayon). **VERIFIED** (UNESCO and Wikipedia architectural/historical details).\n\n**3. Footprint comparison (Borobudur vs. Angkor Wat the temple):**\n\n**Angkor Wat (the temple) is vastly larger by footprint.** Its enclosure covers 162.6 hectares, while Borobudur’s base is roughly 123 m × 123 m ≈ 1.51 hectares (15,129 m²). Angkor Wat’s scale is orders of magnitude greater even when comparing just the temple enclosures.[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angkor_Wat)[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borobudur)\n\n**4. Angkor Wat entry price and additional notes:**\n\nAngkor Wat (part of the Angkor Archaeological Park) requires a 1-day pass for foreigners of **US$37** (as of recent official data; other options: 3-day ~$62, 7-day ~$72). Prices are in USD and apply to foreign tourists. **VERIFIED** (official Angkor Enterprise government site).[[6]](https://www.angkorenterprise.gov.kh/en)[[7]](https://www.siemreap.net/guides/angkor/hours-admission/)\n\nAll claims above draw from the cited sources (UNESCO official pages, Wikipedia with scholarly/archaeological references, consistent secondary confirmations). No conflicting primary data was found in the results.","logprobs":[],"annotations":[{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borobudur","start_index":254,"end_index":300,"title":"1"},{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borobudur","start_index":300,"end_index":346,"title":"1"},{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borobudur","start_index":815,"end_index":861,"title":"1"},{"type":"url_citation","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borobudur","start_index":861,"end_index":907,"title":"1"},{"type":"url_citation","url":"https:/